<info>
  <title>Siliko Infix Language Reference</title>
</info>

<para>Siliko Core includes a built-in infix parser that handles
traditional mathematical expressions. <firstterm>Infix</firstterm>
refers to the fact that, in this notation, the operator is placed
between the operands on which it operates, as opposed to prefix
notation, such as Lisp-style S-expressions, where the operator comes
first, or suffix notation, such as reverse Polish notation, where the
operator comes last. The Siliko infix language also includes support for
function calls, using a syntax where the function name comes first, and
its arguments are passed as a comma-separated list between parentheses
after the function name. The Siliko infix language supports single
mathematical expression without variables or state. Whitespace
characters can be used to separate tokens, but are otherwise
ignored.</para>

<section>
<info>
  <title>Lines</title>
</info>

<mediaobject>
  <imageobject>
    <imagedata align="center" fileref="../svg/line.svg" format="svg" scale="100"/>
  </imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>Every string or characters processed by the infix parser is called
a line. A line consists of an expression, surrounded by whitespace,
ending with a null character. </para>
</section>

<section>
<info>
    <title>Whitespace</title>
</info>

<mediaobject>
  <imageobject>
    <imagedata align="center" fileref="../svg/space.svg" format="svg" scale="100"/>
  </imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>Except to separate tokens, whitespace is ignored.</para>
</section>

<section>
<info>
    <title>Numbers</title>
</info>

<mediaobject>
  <imageobject>
    <imagedata align="center" fileref="../svg/number.svg" format="svg" scale="100"/>
  </imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>A number can be either an <type>integer</type> or a
<type>real</type>. Numbers may optionally be proceeded by a minus
sign (<literal>-</literal>).</para>

<section>
<info>
  <title>Integers</title>
</info>

<mediaobject>
  <imageobject>
    <imagedata align="center" fileref="../svg/integer.svg" format="svg" scale="100"/>
  </imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>An <type>integer</type> is a series of one or more decimal digits.
The tokenizer passes the series of digits to the C standard-library
<function>strtoll</function> function to get the <type>integer</type>
value.</para>
</section>

<section>
<info>
  <title>Real Numbers</title>
</info>

<mediaobject>
  <imageobject>
    <imagedata align="center" fileref="../svg/real.svg" format="svg" scale="100"/>
  </imageobject>
</mediaobject>

<para>A <type>real</type> can take one of two forms. In the first form,
it is is either the strings <literal>e</literal>, or
<literal>pi</literal>. In the second form, it is a series of one or more
decimal digits, followed by a decimal point, optionally followed by one
or more decimal digits. The tokenizer passes the series of characters to
the C standard-library <function>strtof</function> function to get the
<type>real</type> value.</para>

<note>
<para>The use of <function>strtof</function> is a known bug, and will
be fixed before full release.</para>
</note>
</section>
</section>

<section>
<info>
  <title>Operators</title>
</info>

<para>Operators are placed between the operands on which they function.
Siliko’s infix language supports six operators: <literal>+</literal> for
addition, <literal>-</literal> for subtraction, <literal>*</literal> for
multiplication, <literal>/</literal> for division, <literal>^</literal>
for exponentiation, and <literal>d</literal> for die rolls. Because
addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division are left
associative, the infix parser builds syntax trees in reverse when
parsing sequences of these operators. These operators are also
implemented as functions. The infix parser automatically generates
function calls from these operations.</para>

<table>
  <title>Siliko Infix Operations</title>
<tgroup cols="4">
<thead>
  <row>
    <entry>Operations</entry>
    <entry>Operators</entry>
    <entry>Associativity</entry>
    <entry>Function</entry>
  </row>
</thead>
<tbody>
  <row>
    <entry>die roll</entry>
    <entry><literal>d</literal></entry>
    <entry>none</entry>
    <entry><function>dice</function></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>exponentiation</entry>
    <entry><literal>^</literal></entry>
    <entry>right</entry>
    <entry><function>power</function></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>multiplication, division</entry>
    <entry><literal>*</literal>, <literal>/</literal></entry>
    <entry>left</entry>
    <entry><function>multiply</function>,
      <function>divide</function></entry>
  </row>
  <row>
    <entry>addition, subtraction</entry>
    <entry><literal>+</literal>, <literal>-</literal></entry>
    <entry>left</entry>
    <entry><function>add</function>,
      <function>subtract</function></entry>
  </row>
</tbody>
</tgroup>
</table>

<para>Die rolls can only take a positive integer literal as the second
operand, so die rolls can’t be chained like other operands. All other
operators may take arbitrary expressions as their operands.</para>

